{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And Israel said.</b> Before the spirit of prophecy came upon him, Scripture mentioned him by the name Jacob — ״ותחי רוח יעקב״; once that spirit came upon him, it mentioned him by the name Israel — ״ויאמר ישראל״, ״ויסע ישראל״, ״ויאמר אלהים לישראל במראות הלילה״. And the reason the Holy One, blessed be He, called him Jacob — though He Himself had called his name Israel — is that since his journey to Egypt was to be an exile there, He did not mention him by the name of dominion but by the name of lowliness; and after the Holy One, blessed be He, called him by the name Jacob, Scripture also mentions him by that name — ״ויקם יעקב מבאר שבע״, ״את יעקב אביהם״. And as for what is written ״וישאו בני ישראל״ — He wished to mention in this descent both his names, Israel and Jacob, to indicate that even though he is now Jacob in descent, the children are destined to be redeemed and to go out with a high hand and to return to their dominion and greatness from it; and therefore He needed to mention ״בני ישראל״, for Scripture could have said ״וישאו בני יעקב את אביהם״. And this is what is written: ״ואלו שמות בני ישראל הבאים מצרימה יעקב ובניו״ — for those who came to Egypt are Jacob and his sons, and they are destined to be called בני ישראל and to be counted by the number of names." "<b>It is enough; Joseph my son is still alive.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): since they told him two things — ״עוד יוסף חי״ and that he is a ruler — he answered them: what difference does it make to me if he is a ruler? It is enough for me that he is alive." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ״רב עוד יוסף בני חי״ — great reward and abundance of goodness is mine in this world and in the world to come, seeing that Joseph my son is alive. This is like the expression (Psalms 31): ״מה רב טובך״. And he added the word עוד, which indicates the matter for the future, as it is written (Psalms 84): ״עוד יהללוך סלה״." "<b>And it appears to me</b> that Onkelos alluded to this, for he translated ״סגי לי חדוא״ — and there is no abundance of joy except for the soul in the world to come, for the joy of this world is a thing that perishes — all the more so for Jacob, whom troubles found all his days, as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: ״אין הבטחה לצדיקים בעוה\"ב״. And corresponding to what he said when he was lost from him — ״כי ארד אל בני אבל שאולה״ — because the death of his son in his lifetime testifies to his wickedness, and because of this he feared the punishment of Gehenna, which is שאול — now he said, when he saw him alive, that he is assured he will merit ג״ע and the world to come."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {3 "The prooftext אין הבטחה לצדיקים is well known from rabbinic sources (e.g. Bavli Eruvin 22a) as אין הבטחה לצדיקים בעוה\"ז, 'there is no assurance for the righteous in this world.' The text reads בעוה\"ב, 'in the world to come,' which appears to be a scribal error; translated hyper-literally as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:43.977652567Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}